TI  - Renal Cell Carcinoma Presenting As Isolated Malignant Pleural Effusion: A Case 
      Report.
PG  - e73627
LID - 10.7759/cureus.73627 [doi]
LID - e73627
AB  - Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) of the clear cell type is the most common form of 
      renal malignancy. Malignant pleural effusion (MPE) as the initial presentation of 
      RCC is very rare. This case report presents a case of RCC that initially 
      manifested as a pleural effusion, without the typical symptoms of flank pain, 
      hematuria, or a palpable abdominal mass. The patient, a 45-year-old male, arrived 
      with complaints of dyspnea and a dry cough, with no notable medical history or 
      risk factors. Imaging revealed a left-sided pleural effusion, which was 
      identified as MPE based on pleural fluid analysis, cytology, and 
      immunohistochemistry. Subsequent investigation revealed a large, solid cystic 
      mass in the left kidney, with a biopsy confirming RCC. Uniquely, the patient 
      exhibited pleural metastasis without involvement of the lung parenchyma, a rare 
      scenario in RCC that is linked to a poor prognosis. This case emphasizes the 
      variable presentation of RCC and the need to consider malignancy in patients with 
      unexplained pleural effusion. An indwelling pleural catheter was placed for the 
      patient considering the poor prognosis; furthermore, he declined to undergo 
      sessions of palliative chemotherapy and was subsequently lost to follow-up.
