TI  - Feasibility of ambulatory chest drainage and talc pleurodesis for malignant 
      pleural effusion.
PG  - 391
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2025-aw-2266 [doi]
LID - 391
AB  - BACKGROUND: Patients with a malignant pleural effusion (MPE) experience 
      significant morbidity and a significant symptom burden. For some patients the 
      existing options of inpatient chest drain insertion with talc pleurodesis or 
      indwelling pleural catheter (IPC) insertion may not meet their treatment goals. 
      Based on local patient involvement and engagement, we devised a feasibility study 
      to assess whether chest drain insertion and talc pleurodesis was feasible and 
      safe to deliver on an ambulatory basis. METHODS: A protocol for ambulatory drain 
      insertion and talc pleurodesis for management of MPE was devised. A detailed 
      schematic of the proforma is included. The primary outcome was safety and 
      feasibility of the protocol. We established recruitment criteria as: clinically 
      or cytologically diagnosed MPE, World Health Organization performance status of 
      two or less (three if due to breathlessness), estimated life expectancy over 30 
      days and age >18 years. Exclusion criteria were: known non-expansile ipsilateral 
      lung, previous failed ipsilateral pleurodesis, pregnancy or being unable to 
      safely manage in the community as defined by the treating clinician. Our target 
      enrolment was ten cases. RESULTS: Ten eligible patients were recruited. All 
      patients survived to 60 days post protocol completion. Eight patients received 
      the trial protocol on an entirely ambulatory basis, six of whom received talc 
      pleurodesis. There were three hospital admissions affecting two patients during 
      protocol administration. Neither patient was felt to have been admitted as a 
      direct consequence of the trial protocol. There were no other reported 
      complications. Specifically, there were no issues with drain displacement or 
      accidental disconnections during the study period. The intervention pleurodesis 
      failure rate at 60 days for the entire trial cohort was 30%. Three patients 
      required further pleural procedures, all of whom had received talc slurry. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Our project demonstrates that our protocol for chest drain insertion 
      and talc pleurodesis can be safely and feasibly delivered on an ambulatory basis 
      to patients with a MPE. More work is required to establish the success of our 
      ambulatory pathway compared to existing standards of care. Additionally, work is 
      required to assess how we improve the appeal of an ambulatory pathway with both 
      our patients and clinicians in order to offer a deliverable treatment that 
      matches the care priorities of our patients.
