TI  - Use of Indwelling Pleural Catheter for Recurrent Pleural Effusion Due to 
      Postpericardiotomy Syndrome: A Case Report.
PG  - 160-2
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000196 [doi]
AB  - Pleural effusion secondary to postpericardiotomy syndrome (PPS) is a relatively 
      common complication after cardiac surgery. These effusions and syndrome complex 
      usually respond well to anti-inflammatory agents. The use of indwelling pleural 
      catheter (IPC) for nonmalignant recurrent pleural effusions is growing. We report 
      the use of IPC for a case of recurrent pleural effusion due to PPS in a gentleman 
      that could not tolerate anti-inflammatory medications. To our knowledge, there 
      has been no other report of the use of IPC due to recurrent pleural effusion from 
      PPS.
